WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.620
As you know from this series, fluid simulation
techniques that are able to create high fidelity

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.509
video footage are in abundance in computer
graphics research.

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:18.510
These techniques all have their own tradeoffs,
and when we evaluate them, we often use terms

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:23.050
like first or second-order accuracy, which
are mathematical terms.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:27.470
We often have to evaluate these techniques
against each other by means of mathematics,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:33.010
because this way, we can set up consistent
and unbiased comparisons that everyone understands

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.360
and agrees upon.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.590
However, ultimately, in the show business,
what matters is how the viewers perceive the

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:45.350
end result, whether they think it looks fake,
or if it keeps their suspension of disbelief.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:50.050
We have the choice of not only simulation
techniques, but all of them also have their

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.050
own set of parameters.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:56.579
For instance, the higher the resolution of
our simulations, the more high-frequency details

00:00:56.579 --> 00:00:58.090
appear in the footage.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:03.710
However, after a point, increasing the resolution
further is extremely costly, and while we

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.150
know what is to be gained in terms of mathematics,
it is still unknown how well it would do with

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.150
the users.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.610
So the ultimate question is this: what do
I get for my money and time?

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:19.650
This paper provides an exhaustive user study
to answer this question, where the users are

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.541
asked to look at two different simulations
and as a binary choice, tell us which is the

00:01:24.541 --> 00:01:27.710
one they perceived to be closer to the reference.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.570
The reference footage is a real-world video
of a water sloshing in a tank, and the other

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.650
footage that is to be judged is created via
a fluid simulation algorithm.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.220
Turns out that the reference footage can be
almost anything as long as there are some

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.840
splashing and sloshing going on in it.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.750
It also turns out that after a relatively
favorable breaking point which is denoted

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:55.660
by 2x, further increasing the resolution does
not make a significant difference in the user

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.660
scores.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.000
But boy, does it change the computation times!

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.080
So this is why such studies are super useful,
and it's great to see that the accuracy of

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.390
these techniques are measured both mathematically,
and also how convincing they actually look

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:10.600
for users.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.170
Another curious finding is that if we deny
access to the reference footage, we see a

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.940
large change in different responses and a
similar jump in ambiguity.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.660
This means that we are reasonably bad at predicting
the fine details, therefore, if the simulation

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.190
pushes the right buttons, the users will easily
believe it to be correct even if it is far

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.790
away from the ground truth solution.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.270
Here is a matrix with a ton of rendered footage.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:41.380
Horizontally, you see the same thing with
different simulation techniques, and vertically,

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:45.120
we slowly go from transparent above to opaque
below.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.460
To keep things fair and really reveal which
choices are the best bang for the buck, there

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.010
are also comparisons between techniques that
have a similar computation time.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.810
In these cases, the Fluid Implicit Particle.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:03.520
FLIP in short and the Affine Particle in Cell,
are almost unanimously favorable.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.830
These are advanced techniques that combine
particle and grid-based simulations.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.680
I think this is highly useful information
for more time critical applications, so make

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.240
sure to have a look at the paper for details.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.970
There are similar user studies with glossy
and translucent material models and much more

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:20.970
in the paper.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.420
The source code of this project is also available.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

